## Introduction

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm based on the concept of 'objects' that contain data (attributes) and code (methods). C++ supports OOP through classes, objects, encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism. OOP makes code more modular, reusable, and easier to maintain.
## Key Concepts

- Class: A blueprint/template that defines the structure (data members) and behavior (member functions) of objects
- Object: An instance of a class — a concrete entity created from the class blueprint
- Encapsulation: Bundling data and methods together, hiding internal details using access specifiers (private, public, protected)
- Inheritance: A class (child/derived) can inherit properties and methods from another class (parent/base), enabling code reuse
- Polymorphism: Same interface, different behavior — achieved through function overloading, operator overloading, and virtual functions
- Abstraction: Hiding complex implementation details and showing only essential features
- Procedural vs OOP: Procedural focuses on functions and steps. OOP focuses on objects and their interactions.

## Important Formulas

- `Access specifiers: public (accessible anywhere), private (class only), protected (class + derived classes)`
- `Class definition: class ClassName { access: members; };  (note the semicolon)`
- `Object creation: ClassName obj; or ClassName obj(params);`

## Comparison Tables

### Procedural vs Object-Oriented Programming

| Feature | Procedural (C) | OOP (C++) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Focus | Functions and procedures | Objects and classes |
| Data | Global/local variables | Encapsulated in objects |
| Reusability | Function libraries | Inheritance + composition |
| Security | Data is often global | Private/protected access control |
| Example | C, Pascal | C++, Java, Python |
| Approach | Top-down | Bottom-up |

> OOP provides better data security and code reuse than procedural programming.

## Code Examples

### Defining a Class and Creating Objects

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

// Class definition
class Student {
private:
    string name;
    int rollNo;
    float marks;

public:
    // Member function to set data
    void setData(string n, int r, float m) {
        name = n;
        rollNo = r;
        marks = m;
    }

    // Member function to display data
    void display() {
        cout << "Name: " << name << endl;
        cout << "Roll No: " << rollNo << endl;
        cout << "Marks: " << marks << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    // Create objects
    Student s1, s2;

    s1.setData("Yash", 101, 85.5);
    s2.setData("Riya", 102, 92.0);

    s1.display();
    cout << "---" << endl;
    s2.display();

    return 0;
}
```

The class Student has private data members (name, rollNo, marks) and public member functions (setData, display). Objects s1 and s2 are instances of Student, each with their own copy of the data.


## Summary

Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm based on the concept of 'objects' that contain data (attributes) and code (methods). C++ supports OOP through classes, objects, encapsulation, inheritance, and polymorphism. OOP makes code more modular, reusable, and easier to maintain.

codeExamples.code:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Rectangle {
private:
    int length, width;

public:
    // 1. Default constructor
    Rectangle() {
        length = 0;
        width = 0;
        cout << "Default constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // 2. Parameterized constructor
    Rectangle(int l, int w) {
        length = l;
        width = w;
        cout << "Parameterized constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // 3. Copy constructor
    Rectangle(const Rectangle& other) {
        length = other.length;
        width = other.width;
        cout << "Copy constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // Destructor
    ~Rectangle() {
        cout << "Destructor called for (" << length << ", " << width << ")" << endl;
    }

    int area() { return length * width; }
};

int main() {
    Rectangle r1;           // default constructor
    Rectangle r2(5, 3);     // parameterized constructor
    Rectangle r3 = r2;      // copy constructor

    cout << "r1 area: " << r1.area() << endl;  // 0
    cout << "r2 area: " << r2.area() << endl;  // 15
    cout << "r3 area: " << r3.area() << endl;  // 15

    // Destructors called in reverse order: r3, r2, r1
    return 0;
}

