## Introduction

A constructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is created. It initializes the object's data members. A destructor is called automatically when an object is destroyed (goes out of scope). Constructors have the same name as the class; destructors have a ~ prefix.
## Key Concepts

- Constructor: same name as the class, no return type (not even void), called automatically when object is created
- Default constructor: takes no parameters — initializes members with default values
- Parameterized constructor: takes parameters to initialize members with specific values
- Copy constructor: initializes an object by copying another object of the same class
- Destructor: name starts with ~, called automatically when object is destroyed, used for cleanup (freeing memory, closing files)
- Constructor overloading: multiple constructors with different parameter lists
- Only ONE destructor per class — cannot be overloaded

## Important Formulas

- `Constructor: ClassName() { /* init */ }`
- `Parameterized: ClassName(type param) { /* init */ }`
- `Copy: ClassName(const ClassName& other) { /* copy */ }`
- `Destructor: ~ClassName() { /* cleanup */ }`

## Comparison Tables

### Constructor vs Destructor

| Feature | Constructor | Destructor |
| --- | --- | --- |
| When called | Object creation | Object destruction |
| Name | Same as class name | ~ + class name |
| Return type | None (not even void) | None |
| Parameters | Can have parameters | No parameters |
| Overloading | Yes (multiple) | No (only one) |
| Purpose | Initialize members | Cleanup resources |

> Both are called automatically — you never call them directly.

## Code Examples

### All Types of Constructors + Destructor

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Rectangle {
private:
    int length, width;

public:
    // 1. Default constructor
    Rectangle() {
        length = 0;
        width = 0;
        cout << "Default constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // 2. Parameterized constructor
    Rectangle(int l, int w) {
        length = l;
        width = w;
        cout << "Parameterized constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // 3. Copy constructor
    Rectangle(const Rectangle& other) {
        length = other.length;
        width = other.width;
        cout << "Copy constructor called" << endl;
    }

    // Destructor
    ~Rectangle() {
        cout << "Destructor called for (" << length << ", " << width << ")" << endl;
    }

    int area() { return length * width; }
};

int main() {
    Rectangle r1;           // default constructor
    Rectangle r2(5, 3);     // parameterized constructor
    Rectangle r3 = r2;      // copy constructor

    cout << "r1 area: " << r1.area() << endl;  // 0
    cout << "r2 area: " << r2.area() << endl;  // 15
    cout << "r3 area: " << r3.area() << endl;  // 15

    // Destructors called in reverse order: r3, r2, r1
    return 0;
}
```

Three constructors are demonstrated. The destructor is called automatically when each object goes out of scope at the end of main(). Objects are destroyed in reverse order of creation (LIFO).


## Summary

A constructor is a special member function that is automatically called when an object is created. It initializes the object's data members. A destructor is called automatically when an object is destroyed (goes out of scope). Constructors have the same name as the class; destructors have a ~ prefix.

