## Introduction

Inheritance is a mechanism where a new class (derived/child class) acquires the properties and behaviors of an existing class (base/parent class). It enables code reuse, hierarchical classification, and extensibility. The derived class can add new members or override existing ones.
## Key Concepts

- Base class (parent): the class being inherited from
- Derived class (child): the class that inherits from the base class
- Single inheritance: one base class, one derived class
- Multiple inheritance: a class inherits from multiple base classes
- Multilevel inheritance: A → B → C (chain of inheritance)
- Hierarchical inheritance: multiple classes inherit from one base class
- Access modes: public inheritance (public members stay public), protected inheritance, private inheritance
- The derived class has access to public and protected members of the base class, but NOT private members

## Important Formulas

- `Syntax: class Derived : access_mode Base { /* ... */ };`
- `Public inheritance: class Student : public Person { };`
- `Constructor call order: Base constructor → Derived constructor`
- `Destructor call order: Derived destructor → Base destructor`

## Comparison Tables

### Inheritance Access Modes

| Base Member | Public Inheritance | Protected Inheritance | Private Inheritance |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| public | public in derived | protected in derived | private in derived |
| protected | protected in derived | protected in derived | private in derived |
| private | NOT accessible | NOT accessible | NOT accessible |

> Private members of the base class are NEVER directly accessible in the derived class, regardless of inheritance mode.

## Code Examples

### Single Inheritance — Person → Student

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

// Base class
class Person {
protected:  // accessible in derived classes
    string name;
    int age;

public:
    void setPerson(string n, int a) {
        name = n;
        age = a;
    }
    void displayPerson() {
        cout << "Name: " << name << ", Age: " << age;
    }
};

// Derived class — inherits from Person
class Student : public Person {
private:
    int rollNo;
    float marks;

public:
    void setStudent(string n, int a, int r, float m) {
        setPerson(n, a);  // call base class method
        rollNo = r;
        marks = m;
    }

    void displayStudent() {
        displayPerson();  // call base class method
        cout << ", Roll: " << rollNo << ", Marks: " << marks << endl;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s;
    s.setStudent("Yash", 20, 101, 85.5);
    s.displayStudent();
    // Output: Name: Yash, Age: 20, Roll: 101, Marks: 85.5
    return 0;
}
```

Student inherits from Person. It can access the protected members (name, age) and public methods (setPerson, displayPerson) of Person. Student adds its own members (rollNo, marks) and methods (setStudent, displayStudent).


## Summary

Inheritance is a mechanism where a new class (derived/child class) acquires the properties and behaviors of an existing class (base/parent class). It enables code reuse, hierarchical classification, and extensibility. The derived class can add new members or override existing ones.

codeExamples.code:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Base class
class Shape {
public:
    virtual void draw() {  // virtual function
        cout << "Drawing a generic shape" << endl;
    }
    virtual double area() = 0;  // pure virtual — abstract class
};

class Circle : public Shape {
    double radius;
public:
    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}
    void draw() override {  // override base class virtual
        cout << "Drawing a circle" << endl;
    }
    double area() override {
        return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
    }
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
    double length, width;
public:
    Rectangle(double l, double w) : length(l), width(w) {}
    void draw() override {
        cout << "Drawing a rectangle" << endl;
    }
    double area() override {
        return length * width;
    }
};

int main() {
    // Base class pointers pointing to derived class objects
    Shape* s1 = new Circle(5.0);
    Shape* s2 = new Rectangle(4.0, 6.0);

    s1->draw();  // Output: Drawing a circle (runtime dispatch!)
    cout << "Area: " << s1->area() << endl;  // 78.54

    s2->draw();  // Output: Drawing a rectangle
    cout << "Area: " << s2->area() << endl;  // 24.0

    delete s1;
    delete s2;
    return 0;
}

