## Introduction

Polymorphism means 'many forms' — the same function name or operator can behave differently depending on the context. In C++, polymorphism is achieved through function overloading (compile-time), operator overloading (compile-time), and virtual functions (runtime). Runtime polymorphism uses a base class pointer to call derived class methods.
## Key Concepts

- Compile-time polymorphism: resolved at compile time — function overloading and operator overloading
- Runtime polymorphism: resolved at runtime — virtual functions and overriding
- Function Overloading: same function name, different parameter lists (number, type, or order)
- Operator Overloading: redefine what an operator (+, -, [], etc.) does for user-defined types
- Virtual Function: declared with 'virtual' keyword in base class, overridden in derived class
- Override: derived class provides its own implementation of a virtual function
- Base class pointer + virtual function = runtime polymorphism (dynamic dispatch)
- Pure Virtual Function: virtual void func() = 0; — makes the class abstract (cannot be instantiated)

## Important Formulas

- `Virtual: virtual returnType funcName(params) { }`
- `Pure virtual: virtual returnType funcName(params) = 0;`
- `Function overloading: void print(int); void print(float); void print(string);`

## Comparison Tables

### Compile-time vs Runtime Polymorphism

| Feature | Compile-time | Runtime |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanism | Function/operator overloading | Virtual functions |
| Binding | Early binding (at compile) | Late binding (at runtime) |
| Speed | Faster (resolved at compile) | Slightly slower (vtable lookup) |
| Flexibility | Less flexible | More flexible |
| Example | void print(int), void print(char) | virtual void draw() |

> Runtime polymorphism uses a vtable (virtual table) to look up the correct function at runtime.

## Code Examples

### Runtime Polymorphism with Virtual Functions

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Base class
class Shape {
public:
    virtual void draw() {  // virtual function
        cout << "Drawing a generic shape" << endl;
    }
    virtual double area() = 0;  // pure virtual — abstract class
};

class Circle : public Shape {
    double radius;
public:
    Circle(double r) : radius(r) {}
    void draw() override {  // override base class virtual
        cout << "Drawing a circle" << endl;
    }
    double area() override {
        return 3.14159 * radius * radius;
    }
};

class Rectangle : public Shape {
    double length, width;
public:
    Rectangle(double l, double w) : length(l), width(w) {}
    void draw() override {
        cout << "Drawing a rectangle" << endl;
    }
    double area() override {
        return length * width;
    }
};

int main() {
    // Base class pointers pointing to derived class objects
    Shape* s1 = new Circle(5.0);
    Shape* s2 = new Rectangle(4.0, 6.0);

    s1->draw();  // Output: Drawing a circle (runtime dispatch!)
    cout << "Area: " << s1->area() << endl;  // 78.54

    s2->draw();  // Output: Drawing a rectangle
    cout << "Area: " << s2->area() << endl;  // 24.0

    delete s1;
    delete s2;
    return 0;
}
```

Even though s1 and s2 are Shape pointers, the correct draw() and area() methods of Circle and Rectangle are called at runtime because they are declared virtual. This is runtime polymorphism. Shape is abstract (has a pure virtual function area()=0), so you can't create a Shape object directly.


## Summary

Polymorphism means 'many forms' — the same function name or operator can behave differently depending on the context. In C++, polymorphism is achieved through function overloading (compile-time), operator overloading (compile-time), and virtual functions (runtime). Runtime polymorphism uses a base class pointer to call derived class methods.
